},
{
"id": "pmid:42847339",
"epmc_id": "42847339",
"source": "journal",
"server": null,
"pmid": "42847339",
"doi": "10.3354/dao03936",
"title": "Comparative genomics of Photobacterium damselae from Penaeus (Marsupenaeus) japonicus: insights into pathogenicity and adaptation",
"authors": [
"Ragab W",
"Kawato S",
"Rathnapala JMSN",
"Nozaki R",
"Hirono I"
],
"author_string": "Ragab W, Kawato S, Rathnapala JMSN, Nozaki R, Hirono I.",
"journal": "Diseases of aquatic organisms",
"year": 2026,
"date": "2026-10-08",
"field": "te",
"sections": [
{
"label": null,
"text": "Photobacterium damselae subsp. damselae (PDD), a halophilic, Gram-negative bacterium of the family Vibrionaceae, infects a wide range of aquatic organisms. PDD isolates originating from fish have been characterized in detail, but much less is known about those infecting invertebrates. Currently, only 3 complete PDD genomes isolated from Kuruma shrimp Penaeus (Marsupenaeus) japonicus from Japan are publicly available, severely limiting comparative studies of host adaptation. To address this, we present complete, plasmid-resolved genome assemblies of 4 novel PDD strains (TUMSAT-PD1, -PD2, -PD3, and -PD4) isolated from diseased Kuruma shrimp across multiple prefectures in Japan. Phylogenomic analysis reveals that shrimp PDD strains are genetically heterogeneous and closely related to those infecting other aquatic organisms, suggesting that pathogenicity against shrimp arose independently in multiple PDD strains. While core virulence determinants are chromosomally encoded, we identified key plasmid-borne virulence factors that are likely to enhance the pathogenicity of PDD strains. Comparative analysis with 3 publicly available PDD genomes demonstrated both conserved core virulence determinants and strain-specific accessory genes, reflecting local adaptation and evolutionary diversification. Our findings provide the first geographically expanded genomic framework for Kuruma shrimp-associated PDD, integrating 4 novel Japanese isolates with the existing public genomes. This integrated data set demonstrates that PDD exhibits a generalist evolutionary strategy, capable of rapid adaptation to diverse hosts through conserved virulence machinery and mobile genetic elements. This work significantly expands the genomic basis for understanding PDD epidemiology and pathogenesis within aquaculture systems."
}
],
"n_chars": 1821,
"structured": false,
"has_results": true,
"pub_types": [
"Journal Article"
],
"url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/42847339/",
"doi_url": "https://doi.org/10.3354/dao03936",
"is_open_access": false,
"n": 73
},
{
"id": "pmid:38943580",
"epmc_id": "38943580",
"source": "journal",
"server": null,
"pmid": "38943580",
"doi": "10.31857/s0026898424010051",
"title": "[Structure and Evolution of DNA Transposons of the L31 Superfamily in Bivalves]",
"authors": [
"Puzakov MV",
"Puzakova LV"
],
"author_string": "Puzakov MV, Puzakova LV.",
"journal": "Molekuliarnaia biologiia",
"year": 2024,
"date": "2024-01-01",
"field": "te",
"sections": [
{
"label": null,
"text": "The mobile genetic elements IS630/Tc 1/mariner (ITm) are widespread DNA transposons that make a significant contribution to the evolution of eukaryotic genomes. With the start of large-scale application of next-generation sequencing (NGS) technologies and the emergence of many new whole genome sequences of organisms in nucleotide sequence collections, the ITm elements have been identified in most taxa of the eukaryotic tree of life. Although ITm diversity has been studied in detail, new elements are still found, thus expanding the respective DNA transposon group and calling for review of its classification. Bivalve L31 elements were for the first time analyzed in detail to describe their structures, diversity, distribution, and phylogenetic position among the ITm elements. The L31 transposons were found to form an independent superfamily of an ancient origin within the ITm group. Rather high diversity was observed within the L31 clade; i.e., five phylogenetic clusters were identified. In mollusks, the L31 transposons have been detected only in the subclass Autobranchia and predominate in diversity and number in the infraclass Pteriomorphia. A protein encoded by open reading frame 2 (ORF2) was shown to be an integral structural component of almost all full-length L31 elements. The results provide for a better understanding of the evolution of particular ITm transposons. Further study of the L31 transposons in other taxa (cnidarians) and functional investigation of the ORF2 protein product will help to better understand the evolution of DNa transposons, the mechanisms of their horizontal transfer, and their contribution to eukaryotic biodiversity."
}
],
"n_chars": 1673,
"structured": false,
"has_results": true,
"pub_types": [
"Journal Article",
"English Abstract"
],
