"n": 148
},
{
"id": "pmid:41006474",
"epmc_id": "41006474",
"source": "journal",
"server": null,
"pmid": "41006474",
"doi": "10.1038/s41598-025-18278-2",
"title": "Improved circulating tumor DNA identification for detection of esophageal squamous cell carcinoma by enzymatic methyl sequencing and hybrid neural network",
"authors": [
"Shen J",
"Ren Y",
"Mao Z",
"Hu Q",
"Wan Y",
"Du J",
"Lai Y",
"Shao S",
"Zhang L",
"Wu H",
"Li J",
"Ju S",
"Tong X",
"Zhao J",
"Cao L",
"Xiong D",
"Xu C",
"Xu JC",
"Jiang D"
],
"author_string": "Shen J, Ren Y, Mao Z, Hu Q, Wan Y, Du J, Lai Y, Shao S, Zhang L, Wu H, Li J, Ju S, Tong X, Zhao J, Cao L, Xiong D, Xu C, Xu JC, Jiang D.",
"journal": "Scientific reports",
"year": 2025,
"date": "2025-09-26",
"field": "cfdna",
"sections": [
{
"label": null,
"text": "Detection of cancer at early stage can significantly improve the five-year survival rate of patients. Bisulfite-based methylation detection can cause DNA damage, especially in high GC content regions which was associated with the development of cancers. Loss of aberrant methylated CpG sites in cfDNA will lead to the undetectability of certain circulating tumor DNA (ctDNA), consequently may affect the cancer detection. Our study uses enzymatic method to detect whole genome abnormal methylation regions in esophageal squamous cell carcinoma (ESCC). We also provide a pretrained neural network, hybrid of BERT and CNN (BCNN), to identify ctDNA robustly. Maximum posterior probability is utilized to estimate the fraction of ESCC-derived ctDNA in plasma for predicting the risk of ESCC cancer. Our results analysis indicated that enzyme-based whole-genome methylation sequencing retained more longer cfDNA and detected more CpG sites than bisulfite-based method in both gDNA and cfDNA. Enrichment analysis of differentially methylated regions (DMR) showed that top five pathways were associated with ESCC. Compared to traditional models, our BCNN demonstrates the best performance in identifying ctDNA (AUC = 0.970). By estimating the fraction of plasma ctDNA, our BCNN exhibits high accuracy in ESCC detection even at ultralow sequencing depths (AUC = 0.946). Specifically, in the validation cohort, when the specificity is 93.75%, 7 out of 8 early-stage ESCC (TNM Stage I) were identified as positive in our preliminary results. In conclusion, our preliminary results reinforce the idea of employing BCNN as a novel strategy for ESCC early detection in clinical practice. However, to be applied in clinical, further validation with a larger sample size is necessary."
}
],
"n_chars": 1769,
"structured": false,
"has_results": true,
"pub_types": [
"research-article",
"Journal Article"
],
"url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/41006474/",
"doi_url": "https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-025-18278-2",
"is_open_access": true,
"n": 149
},
{
"id": "doi:10.64898/2026.10.08.757030",
"epmc_id": "PPR1338917",
"source": "preprint",
"server": "bioRxiv",
"pmid": null,
"doi": "10.64898/2026.10.08.757030",
"title": "Genome assemblies and annotations of the poneroid ants  Platythyrea mocquerysi  (Ponerinae) and  Stigmatomma oregonense  (Amblyoponinae)",
"authors": [
"LeBoeuf AC",
"Vizueta J",
"Xiong Z",
"Reitsema-Wang Y",
"Brown A",
"Rafanomezantsoa J",
"Hugo H",
"Fisher BL"
],
"author_string": "LeBoeuf AC, Vizueta J, Xiong Z, Reitsema-Wang Y, Brown A, Rafanomezantsoa J, Hugo H, Fisher BL.",
"journal": "bioRxiv",
"year": 2026,
"date": "2026-10-11",
"field": "te",
"sections": [
{
"label": null,
"text": "We present draft genome assemblies for two poneroid ant species with distinctive life history traits: Platythyrea mocquerysi Emery, 1899 (Ponerinae) and Stigmatomma oregonense Wheeler, 1915 (Amblyoponinae). Platythyrea mocquerysi is a Malagasy ant in which larvae produce secretions from a ventral tubercle that are consumed by adults, representing an unusual form of larva-to-adult trophallaxis. Stigmatomma oregonense , a North American “dracula ant,” feeds non-lethally on larval haemolymph. Using PacBio HiFi long-read sequencing, we assembled the P. mocquerysi genome (187 Mb, 307 scaffolds, N50 = 5.26 Mb, BUSCO 99.2% complete) and the S. oregonense genome (262 Mb, 1,189 scaffolds, N50 = 0.84 Mb, BUSCO 98.3% complete). These genomes encode 10,860 and 12,354 protein-coding genes, respectively. Repeat content is higher in S. oregonense than in P. mocquerysi, 54.3% against a lower bound of 15.7%. Most of the difference is in unclassified repeats, which we show to be diverged transposable elements. Comparative analysis with two additional poneroid genomes from closely related species reveals that genome size variation across poneroid ants is largely explained by transposable element dynamics, with notable expansions in several gene families including carboxylesterases, serine proteases, and lipid metabolism genes in Amblyoponinae. These genomes provide essential resources for investigating the molecular basis of unconventional brood management strategies in ants."
}
],
"n_chars": 1481,
"structured": false,
"has_results": true,
"pub_types": [
"Preprint"
],
"url": "https://doi.org/10.64898/2026.10.08.757030",
