"is_open_access": true,
"n": 400
},
{
"id": "pmid:41382221",
"epmc_id": "41382221",
"source": "journal",
"server": null,
"pmid": "41382221",
"doi": "10.1186/s12935-025-04093-2",
"title": "Methylated CfDNA may distinguish between high- and intermediate-risk uveal melanoma: a pilot study",
"authors": [
"Wu M",
"de Bruyn DP",
"Boers RG",
"Beasley AB",
"Hazelaar DM",
"Makrodimitris S",
"Boers JB",
"Vaarwater J",
"de Keizer ROB",
"Verdijk RM",
"Naus NC",
"Paridaens D",
"Wilting SM",
"Gray ES",
"van IJcken WFJ",
"Gribnau J",
"de Klein A",
"Brosens E",
"Kiliç E"
],
"author_string": "Wu M, de Bruyn DP, Boers RG, Beasley AB, Hazelaar DM, Makrodimitris S, Boers JB, Vaarwater J, de Keizer ROB, Verdijk RM, Naus NC, Paridaens D, Wilting SM, Gray ES, van IJcken WFJ, Gribnau J, de Klein A, Brosens E, Kiliç E, Rotterdam Ocular Melanoma Studygroup.",
"journal": "Cancer cell international",
"year": 2025,
"date": "2025-12-11",
"field": "cfdna",
"sections": [
{
"label": "Background",
"text": "Uveal melanoma (UM) is a highly aggressive malignancy with a metastatic risk that depends on the molecular subclass. This subclass can be determined through molecular characterization of tumor-derived tissue. With eye-sparing treatments, tumor tissue is rarely available for molecular testing. We hypothesized that minimal invasive biomarkers such as methylated cell-free DNA (cfDNA) or circulating tumor DNA (ctDNA) can be used for prognosis and monitoring of patients."
},
{
"label": "Methods",
"text": "Plasma cfDNA was isolated from healthy blood donors (HBDs, N = 19) and UM patients (N = 22). Plasma was collected at baseline (localized disease, N = 13) and during follow-up (metastatic disease, N = 9) from independent patients with high metastatic risk (HR, N = 11) (monosomy 3 and/or BAP1-mutated tumor) or intermediate metastatic risk (IR, N = 11) (disomy 3 and/or SF3B1-mutated tumor). Methylation signatures were determined using genome-wide LpnPI-based methylated DNA sequencing (MeD-seq). Samples with a CpG/reads ratio < 20% (N = 3) were excluded. IchorCNA was used to estimate the tumor fraction. cfDNA samples with detectable tumor fraction (N = 2) were analyzed separately from the other cfDNA samples without detectable tumor fraction (N = 18) to reduce noise in downstream analyses. Differentially methylated regions (DMRs) were identified between the following predefined subgroups: UM (N = 11) vs. HBDs (N = 19), and HR (N = 10) vs. IR (N = 7). To visualize clustering, principal component analysis (PCA) and hierarchical clustering was performed on the DMRs with fold change > 2.0. Gene set enrichment analysis (GSEA, Z-score > 2.0 and p < 0.05) was performed to evaluate biological relevance."
},
{
"label": "Results",
"text": "Distinct clustering was observed between UM and HBDs samples, and between HR and IR samples, although outliers were present in the latter comparison. GSEA implicated eight canonical pathways including the S100 Family Signaling Pathway and RAF/MAP kinase cascade, which are linked to tumorigenesis and immune processes."
},
{
"label": "Conclusion",
"text": "This pilot study reports on cfDNA methylation signatures that differentiates UM patients from HBDs, and may distinguish between intermediate and high risk UM subgroups, supporting its prognostic potential. However, its role in monitoring disease progression requires further validation. Independent replication studies are warranted to confirm our findings and evaluate the clinical applicability in UM."
}
],
"n_chars": 2404,
"structured": true,
"has_results": true,
"pub_types": [
"research-article",
"Journal Article"
],
"url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/41382221/",
"doi_url": "https://doi.org/10.1186/s12935-025-04093-2",
"is_open_access": true,
"n": 401,
"text_before_repair": "Uveal melanoma (UM) is a highly aggressive malignancy with a metastatic risk that depends on the molecular subclass. This subclass can be determined through molecular characterization of tumor-derived tissue. With eye-sparing treatments, tumor tissue is rarely available for molecular testing. We hypothesized that minimal invasive biomarkers such as methylated cell-free DNA (cfDNA) or circulating tumor DNA (ctDNA) can be used for prognosis and monitoring of patients. Plasma cfDNA was isolated from healthy blood donors (HBDs, N = 19) and UM patients (N = 22). Plasma was collected at baseline (localized disease, N = 13) and during follow-up (metastatic disease, N = 9) from independent patients with high metastatic risk (HR, N = 11) (monosomy 3 and/or BAP1-mutated tumor) or intermediate metastatic risk (IR, N = 11) (disomy 3 and/or SF3B1-mutated tumor). Methylation signatures were determined using genome-wide LpnPI-based methylated DNA sequencing (MeD-seq). Samples with a CpG/reads ratio 2.0. Gene set enrichment analysis (GSEA, Z-score > 2.0 and p < 0.05) was performed to evaluate biological relevance. Distinct clustering was observed between UM and HBDs samples, and between HR and IR samples, although outliers were present in the latter comparison. GSEA implicated eight canonical pathways including the S100 Family Signaling Pathway and RAF/MAP kinase cascade, which are linked to tumorigenesis and immune processes. This pilot study reports on cfDNA methylation signatures that differentiates UM patients from HBDs, and may distinguish between intermediate and high risk UM subgroups, supporting its prognostic potential. However, its role in monitoring disease progression requires further validation. Independent replication studies are warranted to confirm our findings and evaluate the clinical applicability in UM.",
"text_repaired": true
},
{
"id": "doi:10.64898/2026.10.13.26365472",
"epmc_id": "PPR1343219",
"source": "preprint",
"server": "medRxiv",
"pmid": null,
"doi": "10.64898/2026.10.13.26365472",
"title": "To TTV or Not to TTV? A Combined Urinary Biomarker Approach in Kidney Transplant Recipients Using Torque Teno Virus, Cell-Free DNA, and BK Polyomavirus Measurements",
"authors": [
"Alhadid Y",
"Mamroth M",
"Wang Z",
"Castaneda J",
"Chadha A",
"Vlassov A",
"Chikova AK"
],
"author_string": "Alhadid Y, Mamroth M, Wang Z, Castaneda J, Chadha A, Vlassov A, Chikova AK.",
"journal": "medRxiv",
"year": 2026,
"date": "2026-10-15",
"field": "cfdna",
"sections": [
{
"label": "Background",
"text": "Noninvasive biomarkers capable of detecting kidney allograft injury and distinguishing rejection from infection remain an important clinical need. Torque teno virus (TTV) reflects host immune status, but its utility in urine has not been well established. We evaluated urinary TTV, total cell-free DNA (cfDNA), cfDNA fragmentation, and BK polyomavirus (BKV) as potential biomarkers of kidney transplant rejection and BK polyomavirus–associated nephropathy (BKPyVAN)."
},
{
"label": "Methods",
"text": "Urine samples from 334 kidney transplant recipients were analyzed in association with kidney biopsies performed within 30 days of sample collection. TTV and cfDNA were measured by multiplex quantitative PCR (qPCR), with a subset analyzed by digital PCR (dPCR), and BKV was quantified by qPCR. Associations with biopsy results were evaluated using group comparisons and nominal logistic regression. Changes associated with outcomes of consecutive biopsies were assessed by matched pairs analysis."
},
{
"label": "Results",
"text": "Among 544 unique urine samples, the urinary TTV/cfDNA ratio was significantly lower in rejection than in non-rejection samples (P<0.0001). Logistic regression adjusted for demographic factors confirmed an inverse association between the qPCR-derived TTV/cfDNA metric and rejection (adjusted odds ratio per delta Cq, 0.91; 95% CI, 0.88–0.94; P<0.00001). Significant differences were observed for all types of rejection. Association was confirmed by dPCR: higher TTV was associated with lower rejection odds, whereas higher cfDNA was associated with greater rejection odds. A nominal logistic model incorporating dPCR measurements achieved an AUROC of 0.76 (95% CI, 0.69–0.82). Matched pairs analysis showed a significant decrease in TTV/cfDNA when rejection developed and an increase following recovery from rejection, with no significant changes when biopsy status remained unchanged. BKV levels were strongly associated with BKPyVAN. No correlation between TTV and BKV was observed. Nominal logistic regression integrating TTV, cfDNA, and BKV measurements yielded AUROCs of 0.75, 0.97, and 0.73 for rejection, BKPyVAN, and non-rejection, respectively."
},
{
"label": "Conclusions",
"text": "A reduced urinary TTV/cfDNA ratio is associated with biopsy-defined kidney transplant rejection, and changes in the ratio correlate with transitions in biopsy results. Combined TTV, cfDNA, and BKV measurements showed differential associations with rejection and BKPyVAN, supporting further evaluation as complementary urinary biomarkers."
}
],
"n_chars": 2453,
"structured": true,
"has_results": true,
