"doi": "10.64898/2026.10.18.759982",
"title": "Evolution-Guided Engineering of Non-conserved N-Glycosylation Sites Improves Heterologous Production of Sulfotransferases",
"authors": [
"Li C",
"Li Y",
"Li X",
"Zhao Y",
"Sun J",
"Cao Y",
"Guo X",
"Zheng S",
"Deng J",
"Sheng J"
],
"author_string": "Li C, Li Y, Li X, Zhao Y, Sun J, Cao Y, Guo X, Zheng S, Deng J, Sheng J.",
"journal": "bioRxiv",
"year": 2026,
"date": "2026-10-20",
"field": "evogen",
"sections": [
{
"label": null,
"text": "Escherichia coli features low cost, mature fermentation technology and high recombinant protein output, but the expression of glycosylation-dependent eukaryotic proteins therein is severely limited. Many glycosylated proteins are key tool enzymes for the biosynthesis of various pharmaceutical molecules, such as glycosaminoglycan-related synthases, but there is a lack of universal protein engineering methods to enable the adaptive evolution of these enzymes in E. coli. We established an evolution-guided optimization strategy that targets non-conserved N-glycosylation sites (EGO-NGly). The core of this strategy lies in the phylogeny-guided selective reversion of evolutionarily acquired N-glycosylation sequons to naturally occurring non-glycosylated residues from early-divergent orthologs, thereby improving heterologous production and residual activity stability for selected sulfotransferases in E. coli. Using heparin 3-O-sulfotransferase 1 (3OST-1) as a model enzyme apparatus in eukaryotic organisms, EGO-NGly extended the residual activity half-life by 4.5-fold. Structural analysis and molecular dynamics simulations indicate that greater surface rigidity and remodeling of intramolecular interaction networks contribute to the improved conformational stability of M7 in an aglycosylated host. EGO-NGly was further applied to a panel of 3OST and 6OST homologs, demonstrating its applicability to a subset of the tested enzymes. Furthermore, EGO-NGly can be orthogonally integrated with other complementary protein engineering strategies, as exemplified by its combination with the machine learning-aided Protein Repair One-Stop Service, generating variants with 8-fold and 17-fold extensions in residual activity half-life. Collectively, these results establish EGO-NGly as a practical and potentially cost-effective engineering strategy for improving the prokaryotic expression and functional performance of selected glycosylation-dependent eukaryotic sulfotransferases."
}
],
